### 3. Why This Design?

Zig's concurrency story is intentionally minimal. The language provides OS threads and atomics, and nothing more—no built-in channels, no async/await, no green threads, no runtime scheduler. Why?

**No hidden control flow.** Every thread creation is visible. A `std.Thread.spawn` call is an explicit, heavy operation. There is no syntactic sugar that hides the cost of spawning a goroutine or launching an async task. You see the thread handle; you must explicitly join it. This aligns with the "no hidden control flow" pillar: when reading code, you know that ordinary function calls are synchronous and will not yield to another coroutine behind your back.

**No hidden allocations.** Thread creation allocates an OS stack, but no heap allocations are made by the Zig runtime. There is no per-thread GC, no heap-allocated context for a green thread. You control all memory. If you want a thread pool, you build it (or use a library). This transparency is essential for systems where every allocation counts.

**Explicit data sharing.** By refusing to provide a built-in shared-nothing message-passing model, Zig forces you to confront the reality of concurrent memory access. You must decide how data is shared: raw pointers, atomics, or synchronization primitives. This is not an omission but a feature: it gives you the freedom to pick the exact mechanism that fits the hardware and the problem.

**Portability without abstraction.** OS threads are the lowest common denominator of parallel execution across all modern systems. By sticking to OS threads, Zig's concurrency model is trivially portable and maps directly to the kernel scheduler's concepts (priorities, affinities, real-time scheduling). You can always reach down to the OS-specific APIs (e.g., `std.os.linux.clone` for fine-grained control on Linux) because Zig doesn't hide the system layer.

**Composability.** By providing only low-level primitives, Zig stays out of the way of libraries that build higher-level abstractions. A library can implement an async event loop, actor model, or task pool tailored to a specific domain, using the same building blocks. There is no "function coloring" problem because there is no language-level async. A synchronous function can be called from anywhere; an async library would need its own scheduling, but that's a library concern.

In short, Zig trusts the engineer to understand their performance and safety requirements, and it gives them the knobs without imposing a one-size-fits-all concurrency paradigm.

