### 8. Examples

**Example 1: Ping-pong using release/acquire ordering.**
This demonstrates the essential happens-before pattern. One thread writes a value and sets a flag; the other spins until it sees the flag, then reads the value. Release/acquire ensures the data is visible.

```zig
const std = @import("std");

const Signal = struct {
    data: u64 = 0,
    ready: std.atomic.Atomic(bool) = std.atomic.Atomic(bool).init(false),
};

fn producer(sig: *Signal) void {
    sig.data = 42;
    // Release ensures all prior writes (sig.data) are visible
    // to a thread that acquires this store.
    sig.ready.store(true, .release);
}

fn consumer(sig: *Signal) void {
    // Acquire pairs with the producer's release, establishing
    // that all writes before the release are visible here.
    while (!sig.ready.load(.acquire)) {
        // spin
        std.atomic.spinLoopHint();
    }
    std.debug.assert(sig.data == 42);
}

pub fn main() !void {
    var sig = Signal{};
    const t1 = try std.Thread.spawn(.{}, producer, .{&sig});
    const t2 = try std.Thread.spawn(.{}, consumer, .{&sig});
    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}
```

The `spinLoopHint()` emits a `pause` instruction on x86 or `yield` on ARM, reducing power consumption and improving SMT sibling thread performance.

**Example 2: Spinlock built with atomics.**
Before reaching for `std.Thread.Mutex`, it's instructive to see how a simple spinlock works. This is not production-ready (no fairness, can't handle contention well), but it illustrates acquire/release and atomic exchange.

```zig
const Atomic = std.atomic.Atomic;

const Spinlock = struct {
    flag: Atomic(bool) = Atomic(bool).init(false),

    fn acquire(self: *Spinlock) void {
        while (true) {
            // Try to atomically set the flag to true, obtaining the old value.
            // Use acquire ordering so that the critical section isn't reordered
            // before the lock acquisition.
            if (!self.flag.swap(true, .acquire)) {
                return;
            }
            // Lock was held; spin until it appears false.
            while (self.flag.load(.monotonic)) {
                std.atomic.spinLoopHint();
            }
        }
    }

    fn release(self: *Spinlock) void {
        // Release ensures the critical section's stores are visible
        // before the lock is seen as free.
        self.flag.store(false, .release);
    }
};
```

Usage:

```zig
var lock = Spinlock{};
var shared_value: u64 = 0;

fn worker(lock: *Spinlock) void {
    lock.acquire();
    defer lock.release();
    shared_value += 1;
}
```

While this works, in practice use `std.Thread.Mutex` which integrates with the OS for fairness and blocking. The spinlock demonstrates the atomic underpinnings.

**Example 3: Thread-local counter with atomic aggregation.**
Each thread increments a local counter, then atomically adds to a global total. This minimizes cache coherency traffic compared to incrementing a single global atomic.

```zig
fn countingWorker(global: *Atomic(u64)) void {
    var local: u64 = 0;
    for (0..1_000_000) |_| {
        local += 1;
    }
    _ = global.fetchAdd(local, .monotonic);
}

pub fn main() !void {
    var global = Atomic(u64).init(0);
    var threads: [4]std.Thread = undefined;
    for (0..4) |i| {
        threads[i] = try std.Thread.spawn(.{}, countingWorker, .{&global});
    }
    for (threads) |t| t.join();
    std.debug.print("total = {}\n", .{global.load(.monotonic)});
}
```

This pattern is widely used in concurrent counters (e.g., `perf stat`). The final total is exactly `4 * 1_000_000`.

